Carafe: on Horizon the disk cache stores a block on the thread that compiled it, without a writer thread, appends
where it last wrote, and checks every record before using it.
FEX starts its writer with NtCreateThreadEx at an ARM64 function. On Horizon every new thread of a 32-bit game
starts as an x86 thread, so the writer ran FEX's own code as x86 and the game ended with an access violation in
libwow64fex.dll as soon as the cache was on. Without a writer, Store writes the blob and its index entry at once,
one store at a time, as the writer did.
Each append went where the file's size said the file ended. The file overlay holds writes back from the card, so the
size lagged behind and new records landed on earlier ones: a 180 MB cache had its index chain broken at 18 MB and
records whose entry points and relocations pointed outside their code, and a block taken from it raised an exception
inside the compiler that then hung. Now the files keep their own end, the index file's end is where its last whole
record ends, every record carries a hash of its payload that is checked when the index is read and when a block is
taken, entry points and relocations must fall inside the code, and a record skipped as corrupt no longer leaves the
reader in the middle of it. The cache directory moves, so a cache written before this is not read.
MIT, as the files it changes.

